{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-be49e9a518abbf09", "type": "tool_result", "content": "1\tWhisper-Aware LLM: Self-Supervised Uncertainty Learning for Robust\n2\tWhispered Speech Recognition\n3\tGaopeng Xu1 , Zhenyu Wang1 , Zheng Xue1 , Yinfeng Xia1 , Haitao Yao1,∗∗\n4\t1\n5\t\n6\tQwen Business Unit of Alibaba, China\n7\t\n8\t{longque.xgp, wzy497957, jeremy.xz, xiayinfeng.xyf, timmy.yht}@alibaba-inc.com\n9\t\n10\tarXiv:2608.10836v1 [cs.SD] 11 Aug 2026\n11\t\n12\tAbstract\n13\tThe signal ambiguity of whispered speech drives ASR systems\n14\ttoward two opposing failure modes: failing to capture whispered speech or hallucinatory transcription of noise. This paper\n15\tintroduces the Whisper-Aware LLM, a framework that teaches\n16\tan Audio-LLM to perceive and react to this uncertainty. Our\n17\tmodel develops an intrinsic self-awareness by learning to quantify the physical deficiencies of acoustic signals through targeted self-supervised tasks. This learned uncertainty is then\n18\toperationalized via a novel Confidence-Fused Decoding mechanism, which provides both high-level instructions and framelevel attention modulation to the LLM decoder. Our experiments confirm the effectiveness of this approach. The model\n19\tsets a new state-of-the-art on whispered speech with a 17% relative CER reduction on AISHELL6-Whisper. At the same time,\n20\tit directly addresses the reliability trade-off, with hallucination\n21\trates dropping from over 25% to 4.5%.\n22\tIndex Terms: Whisper speech recognition, Audio-LLM, SelfSupervised learning, Uncertainty estimation\n23\t\n24\t1. Introduction\n25\tWhispered speech, a mode of phonation without vocal fold vibration, presents a formidable and long-standing challenge for\n26\tAutomatic Speech Recognition (ASR) systems. The acoustic\n27\tmanifestation of whispering characterized by the absence of\n28\ta fundamental frequency (F0) and harmonic structure, and a\n29\tnoise-like quality [1, 2] creates a fundamental ambiguity that\n30\tconventional ASR models struggle to resolve. This ambiguity gives rise to a debilitating accuracy-reliability trade-off. On\n31\tone hand, models trained primarily on normal speech exhibit\n32\tperceptual failure when encountering whispers, leading to high\n33\terror rates. On the other hand, increasing a model’s sensitivity\n34\tto capture these faint cues paradoxically heightens its susceptibility to hallucination, where it erroneously transcribes speech\n35\tfrom ambient noise.\n36\tExisting research has attempted to mitigate this trade-off\n37\tvia two principal avenues. The first is a data-centric approach focusing on augmentation, such as generating “pseudowhisper” data [1, 3]. While beneficial, these methods are limited by the inevitable distributional gap between synthetic and\n38\tauthentic whispered speech. The second avenue is modelcentric static adaptation, exemplified by the projection layer\n39\tproposed by Li et al. [2]. While an improvement, such “onesize-fits-all” static solutions fail to dynamically adjust to the\n40\tvast spectrum of whisper variability.\n41\tCrucially, these prevailing paradigms primarily address the\n42\tacoustic symptoms of whispered speech, rather than its underly** indicates the corresponding author.\n43\t\n44\ting cause: a fundamental increase in signal uncertainty. A pivotal acoustic study by Lin et al. [1] experimentally confirmed\n45\tthat the lack of glottal information is the single most critical factor degrading recognition performance. This key finding suggests a new direction: instead of forcing the model to make\n46\thigh-confidence predictions from highly uncertain signals, a\n47\tmore robust approach is to first teach the model to quantify the\n48\tsignal’s intrinsic uncertainty itself.\n49\tThis paper is built upon this principle. We introduce the\n50\tWhisper-Aware LLM, a framework designed to endow a native Audio-LLM with an intrinsic self-awareness of signal uncertainty. We posit that a model can learn to perceive this\n51\tuncertainty in a self-supervised manner by being trained to\n52\tidentify the very physical deficiencies that define whispered\n53\tspeech. To this end, we design two innovative self-supervised\n54\ttasks: F0 contour prediction and masked spectrum reconstruction. The quantified uncertainty is then operationalized through\n55\tour Confidence-Fused Decoding mechanism. To ensure stable\n56\tlearning, we introduce a disciplined three-stage training strategy. Our contributions are:\n57\t1. A novel framework integrating self-supervised uncertainty\n58\tperception directly into an Audio-LLM.\n59\t2. Two physics-informed self-supervised tasks for label-free\n60\tuncertainty learning.\n61\t3. A Confidence-Fused Decoding mechanism for dynamic generative control.\n62\t4. A systematic three-stage training protocol for robust representation learning.\n63\t\n64\t2. The Whisper-Aware LLM Framework\n65\tOur framework enhances a standard Audio-LLM by integrating a lightweight Uncertainty Perception Module (UPM) and a\n66\tcorresponding Confidence-Fused Decoding mechanism.\n67\t2.1. Overall Architecture\n68\tAs shown in Fig.1, the architecture consists of three main\n69\tstages:\n70\t• Acoustic Encoding: The encoder E maps an input audio waveform w into a sequence of representations Henc ∈\n71\tRT ×Dmodel .\n72\t• Uncertainty Perception: Our UPM, U, takes Henc as input\n73\tand produces a global uncertainty vector uglobal and a framewise confidence sequence Hconf .\n74\t• Confidence-Fused Decoding: The decoder D generates the\n75\ttranscription Y autoregressively, guided by the UPM’s outputs.\n76\t\n77\t\fUncertainty Perception Module(UPM)\n78\t\n79\tWI\n80\t\n81\tWhisper Indicator\n82\t\n83\tSR\n84\t\n85\tSpeech Representation\n86\t\n87\tTR\n88\t\n89\tText Representation\n90\t\n91\t[FP0]\n92\t\n93\tWhisper-Aware LLM\n94\t\n95\tFrame-wise\n96\tConfidence\n97\t\n98\tConfidence-Fused\n99\tLLM Decoder(D)\n100\t\n101\tF0 Counter\n102\tPrediction\n103\t\n104\t[FFN]\n105\t\n106\t(��0 )\n107\t\n108\tFeed-Forward\n109\t\n110\t[ENC]\n111\t\n112\tInput Audio\n113\tW\n114\t\n115\t�표 �\n116\t\n117\tAcoustic\n118\tEncoder(E)\n119\t\n120\t[MS]\n121\tMasked Spectrum\n122\tReconstruction\n123\t(�푆푝 )\n124\t\n125\tAttention\n126\tModulation\n127\t(Additive Bais)\n128\t\n129\tGlobal\n130\tUncertainty\n131\tVector\n132\t\n133\t[SA]\n134\tSelf-Attention\n135\tFinal\n136\tTranscription\n137\t\n138\t[IP]\n139\tInput-Processor\n140\t\n141\t�푔푙표\n142\t\n143\t푙\n144\t\n145\tInstruction\n146\tEmbedding\n147\t\n148\tAdapter\n149\t\n150\tAcoustic\n151\tRepresentations\n152\t\n153\tWI\n154\t\n155\tMLP\n156\t\n157\tSR\n158\t\n159\tTR\n160\t\n161\tSpeech\n162\tRepresentation\n163\t\n164\tFigure 1: The overall architecture of the Whisper-Aware LLM. The model first encodes the audio into acoustic representations. Our\n165\tUPM then processes these representations to perceive signal quality, producing a global instruction embedding (einstruct ) and a framewise confidence sequence (Hconf ). These outputs guide the LLM decoder: the instruction acts as a system prompt, and the confidence\n166\tscores modulate the attention mechanism.\n167\t\n168\t2.2. Uncertainty Perception Module (UPM)\n169\t\n170\t2.3. Confidence-Fused Decoding\n171\t\n172\tThe UPM is a small network trained to perceive signal uncertainty through two self-supervised auxiliary tasks.\n173\t\n174\t2.3.1. Global Instruction Embedding\n175\t\n176\t2.2.1. Task 1: F0 Contour Prediction\n177\tMotivated by the finding that F0 absence is the most critical\n178\tfactor in whisper recognition degradation [1], we train a prediction head fF0 within the UPM to predict the F0 contour F̂0\n179\tfrom Henc . The training loss is the Mean Squared Error (MSE)\n180\tagainst a ground-truth contour F0 :\n181\tT\n182\t\n183\tLF0 =\n184\t\n185\t1 X\n186\t(F̂0 (t) − F0 (t))2\n187\tT t=1\n188\t\n189\t(1)\n190\t\n191\tThe frame-wise confidence score hconf (t) is derived from the\n192\tnormalized prediction error eF0 (t):\n193\t\u0012\n194\thconf (t) = 1 − clip\n195\t\n196\t\u0013\n197\teF0 (t)\n198\t, 0, 1\n199\tϵmax\n200\t\n201\t(2)\n202\t\n203\twhere ϵmax is an error clipping hyperparameter. The resulting\n204\tHconf is then centered to have a zero mean.\n205\t\n206\tThe global uncertainty vector uglobal is transformed by an\n207\tMLP[4], finstr , into an instruction embedding einstruct . This is\n208\tprepended to the decoder’s input sequence, acting as a highlevel system prompt.\n209\t2.3.2. Attention Modulation\n210\tIn the LLM-based architecture, the acoustic representations\n211\tHenc are treated as a prefix to the token sequence, and the\n212\tLLM performs causal self-attention over this entire combined\n213\tsequence. To guide this process, we introduce an attention biasing mechanism. For any query q within the LLM, when it\n214\tcomputes an attention score with a key kt that corresponds to\n215\tthe t-th acoustic frame from the encoder, we inject our learned\n216\tconfidence as an additive bias. The modified score is:\n217\tq ⊤ kt\n218\tscore(q, kt ) = √\n219\t+ w · hconf (t)\n220\tdk\n221\t\n222\t(3)\n223\t\n224\twhere w is a learnable scalar. This simple yet effective mechanism allows the model to dynamically pay less attention to\n225\tacoustic frames deemed unreliable by the UPM, preventing the\n226\tdecoder from being misled by uncertain evidence.\n227\t2.4. Three-Stage Training Strategy\n228\t\n229\t2.2.2. Task 2: Masked Spectrum Reconstruction\n230\tTo capture the unstructured, noise-like nature of the whisper\n231\tspectrum, a second head fSpec is trained to reconstruct randomly\n232\tmasked frames in Henc . The UPM’s final hidden state, averaged\n233\tover time, forms the global uncertainty vector uglobal .\n234\t\n235\tTo stably integrate our Uncertainty Perception Module (UPM)\n236\twhile avoiding gradient conflicts with the large pre-trained\n237\tmodel, we employ a disciplined three-stage training strategy:\n238\t• Stage 1: UPM Pre-training. We first train the UPM exclusively on its self-supervised objectives (LF0 + LSpec ), while\n239\t\n240\t\fthe entire Audio-LLM backbone remains frozen. This isolates the UPM, allowing it to rapidly learn robust uncertainty\n241\tfeatures from the frozen encoder without destabilizing the\n242\tmain model.\n243\t• Stage 2: Interface Adaptation. Next, we focus on connecting the UPM to the decoder. We jointly train the audio encoder, the audio-LLM adapter, the UPM, and our new decoding interfaces (the MLP finstr and scalar w), while keeping the\n244\tlarge LLM decoder frozen. This stage aligns the modalities\n245\tand teaches the model to use the uncertainty signals.\n246\t• Stage 3: Full End-to-End Fine-tuning. Finally, the entire model is fine-tuned end-to-end using a composite loss\n247\t(LASR + λaux (LF0 + LSpec )). To maintain efficiency, we apply\n248\tLoRA[5] to the LLM decoder.\n249\t\n250\t3. Experiments\n251\tTo validate the effectiveness of our Whisper-Aware LLM, we\n252\tconducted a series of comprehensive experiments. We aimed to\n253\tevaluate its performance on whispered speech recognition, its\n254\tgeneral ASR capabilities, its robustness against hallucination,\n255\tand the contribution of its core components.\n256\t3.1. Experimental Setup\n257\t3.1.1. Datasets and Metrics\n258\tTraining Datasets. Our work leverages two strategically constructed datasets for different training phases to build a highly\n259\trobust model.\n260\t• UPM Pre-training Corpus (for Stage 1): To ensure the\n261\tUPM learns a comprehensive model of acoustic signals,\n262\twe constructed a large-scale, mixed-domain corpus. This\n263\tcorpus includes General-Purpose Speech (a 3,000h subset\n264\tof WenetSpeech[9], a 3,000h subset of GigaSpeech[10],\n265\tAISHELL-1[11] and LibriSpeech[12]), Whispered Speech\n266\t(wTIMIT[13], AISHELL6-Whisper[14]), and a 1,000h collection of pure noise.\n267\t• Fine-tuning Set (for Stage 2 & 3): This set consists of\n268\tthe training splits of AISHELL-1, LibriSpeech, wTIMIT,\n269\tAISHELL6-Whisper, and a 200h subset of the pure noise\n270\tdata to maintain robustness.\n271\tEvaluation Datasets. We evaluate our model’s performance across three key dimensions:\n272\t• Whispered Speech: The official test sets of AISHELL6Whisper [2] (Chinese) and wTIMIT [15] (English).\n273\t• General-Purpose ASR: The test sets of AISHELL-1 and LibriSpeech to assess general competency.\n274\t• Reliability and Hallucination: To specifically evaluate hallucination under adverse conditions, we created a Noise Hallucination Set containing 1000 audio clips. These clips consist\n275\tof various non-speech sounds (e.g., machine hum, wind) and\n276\tsegments with either no speech or very faint speech heavily\n277\tmasked by noise, designed to be challenging for conventional\n278\tVADs and prone to trigger false transcriptions.\n279\tMetrics. The primary performance metrics are Character Error Rate (CER%) for Chinese and Word Error Rate\n280\t(WER%)/CER% for English. On the Noise Hallucination Set,\n281\twe measure the Hallucination Rate (HR%), calculated as the\n282\tpercentage of audio files containing no transcribable speech that\n283\tproduce a non-empty transcription.\n284\t\n285\t3.1.2. Implementation Details\n286\t• Base Model: Our framework is built upon the Qwen2-Audio\n287\tmodel [16]. It consists of an audio encoder, a lightweight\n288\tprojector as the audio-LLM adapter, and a Qwen-7B LLM\n289\t[17] backbone.\n290\t• Uncertainty Perception Module (UPM): Our UPM is composed of a shared feature extractor (two 1D-CNN layers with\n291\tGeLU and a single Transformer[18] encoder layer) followed\n292\tby two task-specific heads for F0 prediction and masked\n293\tspectrum reconstruction.\n294\t• Training Details: Our three-stage strategy was implemented\n295\tusing the AdamW[19] optimizer with a learning rate of\n296\t1×10−5 . In Stage 3, the LLM was updated via LoRA\n297\t(rank=64, alpha=32). The auxiliary loss weight was set to\n298\tλaux = 0.1. Stage 1 ran for 100k steps, while Stages 2 and 3\n299\teach ran for 20k steps.\n300\t3.1.3. Compared Systems\n301\tWe benchmark against internal baselines sharing the Qwen2Audio backbone and external state-of-the-art (SOTA) systems.\n302\tThe systems S1-S7 are defined as: Whisper-v3 [20] (S1) and\n303\tits fine-tuned version (S2); a fine-tuned Qwen2-Audio baseline\n304\t(S3); and other recent SOTA ASR systems (S4-S7).\n305\t3.2. Main Results\n306\t3.2.1. Whispered Speech Accuracy\n307\tWe first evaluate whispered speech accuracy on both Chinese\n308\tand English benchmarks to demonstrate the cross-lingual effectiveness of our framework.\n309\tAs detailed in Table 2, our model achieves a new state-ofthe-art on the challenging AISHELL6-Whisper test set. With a\n310\tCER of only 1.31% on whispered speech, it significantly surpasses all compared systems, including the strong Seed-ASR\n311\t(S7) which scored 1.58%. This represents a relative CER reduction of 17% over the previous best. Importantly, our model\n312\talso achieves the lowest error rate on normal speech (0.63%),\n313\tindicating that its specialization for whispers does not compromise its general ASR capabilities.\n314\tThe results on English benchmarks, presented in Table 1,\n315\tfurther reinforce these findings. Our model consistently outperforms all baselines across both normal (N) and whispered\n316\t(W) conditions for US and Singaporean (SG) accents. Notably,\n317\twhile other models show a dramatic increase in WER when\n318\ttransitioning from normal to whispered speech, our model exhibits a much more graceful degradation. This demonstrates its\n319\tenhanced robustness to the acoustic shift caused by whispering,\n320\ta direct result of its uncertainty-aware architecture.\n321\t3.2.2. General ASR Performance\n322\tTo explicitly verify that our model’s specialization for whispers does not impair its general capabilities, we summarize its\n323\tperformance on standard, non-whispered benchmarks in Table\n324\t3. Our model demonstrates highly competitive performance\n325\tagainst state-of-the-art systems. On the AISHELL-1 test set,\n326\tit achieves a strong CER of 1.34%, placing it on par with other\n327\ttop-tier systems like Funasr (1.22%). On the LibriSpeech-clean\n328\tset, our model obtains a robust WER of 1.91%, again showing performance in the same league as leading models. This\n329\tconfirms that our uncertainty-aware framework successfully enhances robustness in challenging conditions without sacrificing\n330\tits proficiency on high-quality, normal speech.\n331\t\n332\t\fTable 1: Detailed performance comparison on English benchmarks (WER%/CER%). Our model demonstrates superior performance\n333\ton both normal (N) and whispered (W) speech.\n334\tWER (%)\n335\t\n336\tModel\n337\t\n338\tCER (%)\n339\t\n340\tNU S\n341\t\n342\tNSG\n343\t\n344\tWU S\n345\t\n346\tWSG\n347\t\n348\tNU S\n349\t\n350\tNSG\n351\t\n352\tWU S\n353\t\n354\tWSG\n355\t\n356\tS1: Whisper-v3\n357\tS2: Whisper-v3 fine-tuned\n358\tS3: Qwen2-Audio fine-tuned\n359\tS4: Aishell6-whisper AVSR\n360\tS5: Qwen3-ASR[6]\n361\tS6: Funasr-ASR[7]\n362\tS7: Seed-ASR[8]\n363\t\n364\t5.25\n365\t5.58\n366\t4.95\n367\t4.50\n368\t4.99\n369\t4.65\n370\t4.29\n371\t\n372\t8.67\n373\t7.46\n374\t6.85\n375\t6.06\n376\t7.26\n377\t6.96\n378\t6.51\n379\t\n380\t8.20\n381\t7.95\n382\t7.40\n383\t6.35\n384\t7.01\n385\t8.48\n386\t6.88\n387\t\n388\t19.03\n389\t15.79\n390\t13.50\n391\t11.63\n392\t16.46\n393\t19.29\n394\t12.95\n395\t\n396\t1.03\n397\t0.93\n398\t0.88\n399\t0.73\n400\t0.98\n401\t0.91\n402\t0.85\n403\t\n404\t2.50\n405\t1.86\n406\t1.65\n407\t1.33\n408\t1.85\n409\t1.76\n410\t1.62\n411\t\n412\t2.47\n413\t2.37\n414\t2.30\n415\t1.95\n416\t2.45\n417\t2.88\n418\t2.15\n419\t\n420\t8.85\n421\t7.53\n422\t6.20\n423\t5.17\n424\t7.11\n425\t8.24\n426\t6.03\n427\t\n428\tOurs\n429\t\n430\t4.15\n431\t\n432\t5.89\n433\t\n434\t5.92\n435\t\n436\t10.81\n437\t\n438\t0.70\n439\t\n440\t1.25\n441\t\n442\t1.85\n443\t\n444\t4.98\n445\t\n446\tTable 2: Performance on the AISHELL6-Whisper set (CER%).\n447\tCER (%)\n448\t\n449\tModel\n450\t\n451\tNormal\n452\t\n453\tWhisper\n454\t\n455\tS1: Whisper-v3\n456\tS2: Whisper-v3 fine-tuned\n457\tS3: Qwen2-Audio fine-tuned\n458\tS4: Aishell6-whisper AVSR\n459\tS5: Qwen3-ASR\n460\tS6: Funasr-ASR\n461\tS7: Seed-ASR\n462\t\n463\t3.95\n464\t1.62\n465\t1.05\n466\t1.11\n467\t0.64\n468\t0.72\n469\t0.65\n470\t\n471\t18.93\n472\t6.69\n473\t3.98\n474\t4.13\n475\t3.79\n476\t19.50\n477\t1.58\n478\t\n479\tOurs\n480\t\n481\t0.63\n482\t\n483\t1.31\n484\t\n485\tTable 3: General ASR performance on standard benchmarks.\n486\tModel\n487\t\n488\tAISHELL-1\n489\t(CER%)\n490\t\n491\tLibriSpeech-clean\n492\t(WER%)\n493\t\n494\tWhisper-v3\n495\tSeed-ASR\n496\tFunasr\n497\t\n498\t4.72\n499\t1.63\n500\t1.22\n501\t\n502\t1.86\n503\t2.80\n504\t1.51\n505\t\n506\tOurs\n507\t\n508\t1.34\n509\t\n510\t1.91\n511\t\n512\tTable 4: Reliability evaluation on the Noise Hallucination Set.\n513\tModel\n514\t\n515\tHR(%)\n516\t\n517\tS3: Qwen2-Audio fine-tuned\n518\tS5: Qwen3-ASR\n519\tS6: Funasr-ASR\n520\tS7: Seed-ASR\n521\t\n522\t35.5\n523\t25.2\n524\t29.3\n525\t25.2\n526\t\n527\tOurs\n528\t\n529\t4.5\n530\t\n531\t3.3. Ablation of Model Components\n532\tTo dissect the contribution of our proposed components, we\n533\tconducted an ablation study on the AISHELL6-Whisper, with\n534\tresults in Table 5. Starting from the S3 baseline (3.98%\n535\tCER), using only the frame-wise Attention Modulation yields a\n536\tclear improvement to 3.45%. The Global Instruction, however,\n537\tproves to be the primary driver of performance, slashing the\n538\tCER to 1.84% by providing high-level, strategic awareness of\n539\tsignal quality. Finally, the Full Model combines both, achieving\n540\tthe best performance at 1.31%.\n541\tTable 5: Ablation of Confidence-Fused Decoding components\n542\ton AISHELL6-Whisper (Whisper subset).\n543\tConfiguration\n544\t\n545\t3.2.3. Reliability and Hallucination\n546\tA core contribution of our work is resolving the accuracyreliability trade-off. We test this on our curated Noise Hallucination Set, where an ideal system should produce no output.\n547\tAs shown in Table 4, standard fine-tuned models exhibit a high\n548\tHallucination Rate (HR). For instance, even strong baselines\n549\tlike S7 and S5 incorrectly generate text for over 25% of the\n550\tfiles, with the highest rate reaching 29.3% (S6). This highlights\n551\ttheir tendency to “imagine” speech when faced with ambiguous\n552\tsignals. In stark contrast, our Whisper-Aware LLM achieves\n553\tan HR of only 4.5%, a substantial reduction. This remarkable\n554\timprovement demonstrates that by learning to perceive signal\n555\tuncertainty, our model effectively learns when not to speak,\n556\tdrastically mitigating the risk of generating spurious text from\n557\tnon-speech audio. This reduction is primarily attributed to the\n558\tGlobal Instruction mechanism, which guides the decoder, as\n559\tquantified in our ablation study.\n560\t\n561\tBaseline (Vanilla Finetuning)\n562\t+ Attention Modulation Only\n563\t+ Global Instruction Only\n564\t+ Full Model (Ours)\n565\t\n566\tCER(%)\n567\t3.98\n568\t3.45\n569\t1.84\n570\t1.31\n571\t\n572\t4. Conclusion\n573\tIn this paper, we introduced the Whisper-Aware LLM, a framework designed to handle the uncertainty inherent in whispered\n574\tspeech. Instead of just matching patterns, our approach teaches\n575\tthe model to first perceive signal quality through targeted selfsupervised tasks. This learned awareness is then used by our\n576\tConfidence-Fused Decoding mechanism to guide the LLM, allowing it to selectively trust the acoustic evidence it receives.\n577\tOur experiments confirm the success of this strategy. The model\n578\tachieves state-of-the-art results on whispered speech benchmarks and drastically reduces hallucinations, all while remaining a highly effective general-purpose ASR system.\n579\t\n580\t\f5. Generative AI Use Disclosure\n581\tThe authors used generative AI tools solely for language editing\n582\tand grammar polishing. All technical content, methodology,\n583\texperimental design, and analysis were conducted entirely by\n584\tthe authors.\n585\t\n586\t6. References\n587\t[1] Z. Lin, T. Patel, and O. Scharenborg, “Improving whispered\n588\tspeech recognition performance using pseudo-whispered based\n589\tdata augmentation,” in 2023 IEEE Workshop on Automatic Speech\n590\tRecognition and Understanding (ASRU), 2023, pp. 1–8.\n591\t[2] C. Li, F. Su, J. Liu, H. Bu, Y. Wan, H. Suo, and M. Li,\n592\t“Aishell6-whisper: A chinese mandarin audio-visual whisper\n593\tspeech dataset with speech recognition baselines,” arXiv preprint\n594\tarXiv:2403.19833, 2024.\n595\t[3] P. R. Gudepu, G. P. Vadisetti, A. Niranjan, K. Saranu, R. Sarma,\n596\tM. A. B. Shaik, and P. Paramasivam, “Whisper augmented endto-end/hybrid speech recognition system—cyclegan approac,” in\n597\tProc. Interspeech 2020, 2020, pp. 2302–2306.\n598\t[4] D. E. Rumelhart, G. E. Hinton, and R. J. Williams, “Learning\n599\trepresentations by back-propagating errors,” nature, vol. 323, no.\n600\t6088, pp. 533–536, 1986.\n601\t[5] E. J. Hu, Y. Shen, P. Wallis, Z. Allen-Zhu, Y. Li, S. Wang,\n602\tL. Wang, W. Chen et al., “Lora: Low-rank adaptation of large\n603\tlanguage models.” Iclr, vol. 1, no. 2, p. 3, 2022.\n604\t[6] X. Shi, X. Wang, Z. Guo, Y. Wang, P. Zhang, X. Zhang, Z. Guo,\n605\tH. Hao, Y. Xi, B. Yang et al., “Qwen3-asr technical report,” arXiv\n606\tpreprint arXiv:2601.21337, 2025.\n607\t[7] K. An, Y. Chen, Z. Chen, C. Deng, Z. Du, C. Gao, Z. Gao,\n608\tB. Gong, X. Li, Y. Li et al., “Fun-asr technical report,” arXiv\n609\tpreprint arXiv:2509.12508, 2025.\n610\t[8] Y. Bai, J. Chen, J. Chen, W. Chen, Z. Chen, C. Ding, L. Dong,\n611\tQ. Dong, Y. Du, K. Gao et al., “Seed-asr: Understanding diverse\n612\tspeech and contexts with llm-based speech recognition,” arXiv\n613\tpreprint arXiv:2407.04675, 2024.\n614\t[9] B. Zhang, H. Lv, P. Guo, Q. Shao, C. Yang, L. Xie, X. Xu, H. Bu,\n615\tX. Chen, C. Zeng et al., “Wenetspeech: A 10000+ hours multidomain mandarin corpus for speech recognition,” in ICASSP\n616\t2022-2022 IEEE International Conference on Acoustics, Speech\n617\tand Signal Processing (ICASSP). IEEE, 2022, pp. 6182–6186.\n618\t[10] G. Chen, S. Chai, G. Wang, J. Du, W.-Q. Zhang, C. Weng, D. Su,\n619\tD. Povey, J. Trmal, J. Zhang et al., “Gigaspeech: An evolving,\n620\tmulti-domain asr corpus with 10,000 hours of transcribed audio,”\n621\tarXiv preprint arXiv:2106.06909, 2021.\n622\t[11] H. Bu, J. Du, X. Na, B. Wu, and H. Zheng, “Aishell-1: An opensource mandarin speech corpus and a speech recognition baseline,” in 2017 20th conference of the oriental chapter of the international coordinating committee on speech databases and speech\n623\tI/O systems and assessment (O-COCOSDA). IEEE, 2017, pp.\n624\t1–5.\n625\t[12] V. Panayotov, G. Chen, D. Povey, and S. Khudanpur, “Librispeech: an asr corpus based on public domain audio books,”\n626\tin 2015 IEEE international conference on acoustics, speech and\n627\tsignal processing (ICASSP). IEEE, 2015, pp. 5206–5210.\n628\t[13] B. P. Lim, Computational differences between whispered and nonwhispered speech. University of Illinois at Urbana-Champaign,\n629\t2011.\n630\t[14] C. Li, F. Su, J. Liu, H. Bu, Y. Wan, H. Suo, and M. Li,\n631\t“Aishell6-whisper: A chinese mandarin audio-visual whisper\n632\tspeech dataset with speech recognition baselines,” arXiv preprint\n633\tarXiv:2509.23833, 2025.\n634\t[15] B. P. Lim, “Computational differences between whispered and\n635\tnon-whispered speech,” Ph.D. dissertation, University of Illinois\n636\tat Urbana-Champaign, 2011.\n637\t\n638\t[16] Y. Chu, J. Xu, Q. Yang, H. Wei, X. Wei, Z. Guo, Y. Leng, Y. Lv,\n639\tJ. He, J. Lin et al., “Qwen2-audio technical report,” arXiv preprint\n640\tarXiv:2407.10759, 2024.\n641\t[17] J. Bai, S. Bai, Y. Chu, Z. Cui, K. Dang, X. Deng, Y. Fan, W. Ge,\n642\tY. Han, F. Huang et al., “Qwen technical report,” arXiv preprint\n643\tarXiv:2309.16609, 2023.\n644\t[18] A. Vaswani, N. Shazeer, N. Parmar, J. Uszkoreit, L. Jones, A. N.\n645\tGomez, Ł. Kaiser, and I. Polosukhin, “Attention is all you need,”\n646\tAdvances in neural information processing systems, vol. 30, 2017.\n647\t[19] I. Loshchilov and F. Hutter, “Decoupled weight decay regularization,” arXiv preprint arXiv:1711.05101, 2017.\n648\t[20] A. Radford, J. W. Kim, T. Xu, G. Brockman, C. McLeavey, and\n649\tI. Sutskever, “Robust speech recognition via large-scale weak\n650\tsupervision,” in International conference on machine learning.\n651\tPMLR, 2023, pp. 28 492–28 518.\n652\t\n653\t\f\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "90fdb48f-37ba-4f06-8730-497691fd6d3a", "uuid": "ce108a58-5dd1-424f-9c44-6f00bf7070eb", "timestamp": "2026-08-12T08:26:37.555Z", "tool_use_result": {"type": "text", "file": {"filePath": "/tmp/paper_2608.10836.txt", "content": "Whisper-Aware LLM: Self-Supervised Uncertainty Learning for Robust\nWhispered Speech Recognition\nGaopeng Xu1 , Zhenyu Wang1 , Zheng Xue1 , Yinfeng Xia1 , Haitao Yao1,∗∗\n1\n\nQwen Business Unit of Alibaba, China\n\n{longque.xgp, wzy497957, jeremy.xz, xiayinfeng.xyf, timmy.yht}@alibaba-inc.com\n\narXiv:2608.10836v1 [cs.SD] 11 Aug 2026\n\nAbstract\nThe signal ambiguity of whispered speech drives ASR systems\ntoward two opposing failure modes: failing to capture whispered speech or hallucinatory transcription of noise. This paper\nintroduces the Whisper-Aware LLM, a framework that teaches\nan Audio-LLM to perceive and react to this uncertainty. Our\nmodel develops an intrinsic self-awareness by learning to quantify the physical deficiencies of acoustic signals through targeted self-supervised tasks. This learned uncertainty is then\noperationalized via a novel Confidence-Fused Decoding mechanism, which provides both high-level instructions and framelevel attention modulation to the LLM decoder. Our experiments confirm the effectiveness of this approach. The model\nsets a new state-of-the-art on whispered speech with a 17% relative CER reduction on AISHELL6-Whisper. At the same time,\nit directly addresses the reliability trade-off, with hallucination\nrates dropping from over 25% to 4.5%.\nIndex Terms: Whisper speech recognition, Audio-LLM, SelfSupervised learning, Uncertainty estimation\n\n1. Introduction\nWhispered speech, a mode of phonation without vocal fold vibration, presents a formidable and long-standing challenge for\nAutomatic Speech Recognition (ASR) systems. The acoustic\nmanifestation of whispering characterized by the absence of\na fundamental frequency (F0) and harmonic structure, and a\nnoise-like quality [1, 2] creates a fundamental ambiguity that\nconventional ASR models struggle to resolve. This ambiguity gives rise to a debilitating accuracy-reliability trade-off. On\none hand, models trained primarily on normal speech exhibit\nperceptual failure when encountering whispers, leading to high\nerror rates. On the other hand, increasing a model’s sensitivity\nto capture these faint cues paradoxically heightens its susceptibility to hallucination, where it erroneously transcribes speech\nfrom ambient noise.\nExisting research has attempted to mitigate this trade-off\nvia two principal avenues. The first is a data-centric approach focusing on augmentation, such as generating “pseudowhisper” data [1, 3]. While beneficial, these methods are limited by the inevitable distributional gap between synthetic and\nauthentic whispered speech. The second avenue is modelcentric static adaptation, exemplified by the projection layer\nproposed by Li et al. [2]. While an improvement, such “onesize-fits-all” static solutions fail to dynamically adjust to the\nvast spectrum of whisper variability.\nCrucially, these prevailing paradigms primarily address the\nacoustic symptoms of whispered speech, rather than its underly** indicates the corresponding author.\n\ning cause: a fundamental increase in signal uncertainty. A pivotal acoustic study by Lin et al. [1] experimentally confirmed\nthat the lack of glottal information is the single most critical factor degrading recognition performance. This key finding suggests a new direction: instead of forcing the model to make\nhigh-confidence predictions from highly uncertain signals, a\nmore robust approach is to first teach the model to quantify the\nsignal’s intrinsic uncertainty itself.\nThis paper is built upon this principle. We introduce the\nWhisper-Aware LLM, a framework designed to endow a native Audio-LLM with an intrinsic self-awareness of signal uncertainty. We posit that a model can learn to perceive this\nuncertainty in a self-supervised manner by being trained to\nidentify the very physical deficiencies that define whispered\nspeech. To this end, we design two innovative self-supervised\ntasks: F0 contour prediction and masked spectrum reconstruction. The quantified uncertainty is then operationalized through\nour Confidence-Fused Decoding mechanism. To ensure stable\nlearning, we introduce a disciplined three-stage training strategy. Our contributions are:\n1. A novel framework integrating self-supervised uncertainty\nperception directly into an Audio-LLM.\n2. Two physics-informed self-supervised tasks for label-free\nuncertainty learning.\n3. A Confidence-Fused Decoding mechanism for dynamic generative control.\n4. A systematic three-stage training protocol for robust representation learning.\n\n2. The Whisper-Aware LLM Framework\nOur framework enhances a standard Audio-LLM by integrating a lightweight Uncertainty Perception Module (UPM) and a\ncorresponding Confidence-Fused Decoding mechanism.\n2.1. Overall Architecture\nAs shown in Fig.1, the architecture consists of three main\nstages:\n• Acoustic Encoding: The encoder E maps an input audio waveform w into a sequence of representations Henc ∈\nRT ×Dmodel .\n• Uncertainty Perception: Our UPM, U, takes Henc as input\nand produces a global uncertainty vector uglobal and a framewise confidence sequence Hconf .\n• Confidence-Fused Decoding: The decoder D generates the\ntranscription Y autoregressively, guided by the UPM’s outputs.\n\n\fUncertainty Perception Module(UPM)\n\nWI\n\nWhisper Indicator\n\nSR\n\nSpeech Representation\n\nTR\n\nText Representation\n\n[FP0]\n\nWhisper-Aware LLM\n\nFrame-wise\nConfidence\n\nConfidence-Fused\nLLM Decoder(D)\n\nF0 Counter\nPrediction\n\n[FFN]\n\n(��0 )\n\nFeed-Forward\n\n[ENC]\n\nInput Audio\nW\n\n�표 �\n\nAcoustic\nEncoder(E)\n\n[MS]\nMasked Spectrum\nReconstruction\n(�푆푝 )\n\nAttention\nModulation\n(Additive Bais)\n\nGlobal\nUncertainty\nVector\n\n[SA]\nSelf-Attention\nFinal\nTranscription\n\n[IP]\nInput-Processor\n\n�푔푙표\n\n푙\n\nInstruction\nEmbedding\n\nAdapter\n\nAcoustic\nRepresentations\n\nWI\n\nMLP\n\nSR\n\nTR\n\nSpeech\nRepresentation\n\nFigure 1: The overall architecture of the Whisper-Aware LLM. The model first encodes the audio into acoustic representations. Our\nUPM then processes these representations to perceive signal quality, producing a global instruction embedding (einstruct ) and a framewise confidence sequence (Hconf ). These outputs guide the LLM decoder: the instruction acts as a system prompt, and the confidence\nscores modulate the attention mechanism.\n\n2.2. Uncertainty Perception Module (UPM)\n\n2.3. Confidence-Fused Decoding\n\nThe UPM is a small network trained to perceive signal uncertainty through two self-supervised auxiliary tasks.\n\n2.3.1. Global Instruction Embedding\n\n2.2.1. Task 1: F0 Contour Prediction\nMotivated by the finding that F0 absence is the most critical\nfactor in whisper recognition degradation [1], we train a prediction head fF0 within the UPM to predict the F0 contour F̂0\nfrom Henc . The training loss is the Mean Squared Error (MSE)\nagainst a ground-truth contour F0 :\nT\n\nLF0 =\n\n1 X\n(F̂0 (t) − F0 (t))2\nT t=1\n\n(1)\n\nThe frame-wise confidence score hconf (t) is derived from the\nnormalized prediction error eF0 (t):\n\u0012\nhconf (t) = 1 − clip\n\n\u0013\neF0 (t)\n, 0, 1\nϵmax\n\n(2)\n\nwhere ϵmax is an error clipping hyperparameter. The resulting\nHconf is then centered to have a zero mean.\n\nThe global uncertainty vector uglobal is transformed by an\nMLP[4], finstr , into an instruction embedding einstruct . This is\nprepended to the decoder’s input sequence, acting as a highlevel system prompt.\n2.3.2. Attention Modulation\nIn the LLM-based architecture, the acoustic representations\nHenc are treated as a prefix to the token sequence, and the\nLLM performs causal self-attention over this entire combined\nsequence. To guide this process, we introduce an attention biasing mechanism. For any query q within the LLM, when it\ncomputes an attention score with a key kt that corresponds to\nthe t-th acoustic frame from the encoder, we inject our learned\nconfidence as an additive bias. The modified score is:\nq ⊤ kt\nscore(q, kt ) = √\n+ w · hconf (t)\ndk\n\n(3)\n\nwhere w is a learnable scalar. This simple yet effective mechanism allows the model to dynamically pay less attention to\nacoustic frames deemed unreliable by the UPM, preventing the\ndecoder from being misled by uncertain evidence.\n2.4. Three-Stage Training Strategy\n\n2.2.2. Task 2: Masked Spectrum Reconstruction\nTo capture the unstructured, noise-like nature of the whisper\nspectrum, a second head fSpec is trained to reconstruct randomly\nmasked frames in Henc . The UPM’s final hidden state, averaged\nover time, forms the global uncertainty vector uglobal .\n\nTo stably integrate our Uncertainty Perception Module (UPM)\nwhile avoiding gradient conflicts with the large pre-trained\nmodel, we employ a disciplined three-stage training strategy:\n• Stage 1: UPM Pre-training. We first train the UPM exclusively on its self-supervised objectives (LF0 + LSpec ), while\n\n\fthe entire Audio-LLM backbone remains frozen. This isolates the UPM, allowing it to rapidly learn robust uncertainty\nfeatures from the frozen encoder without destabilizing the\nmain model.\n• Stage 2: Interface Adaptation. Next, we focus on connecting the UPM to the decoder. We jointly train the audio encoder, the audio-LLM adapter, the UPM, and our new decoding interfaces (the MLP finstr and scalar w), while keeping the\nlarge LLM decoder frozen. This stage aligns the modalities\nand teaches the model to use the uncertainty signals.\n• Stage 3: Full End-to-End Fine-tuning. Finally, the entire model is fine-tuned end-to-end using a composite loss\n(LASR + λaux (LF0 + LSpec )). To maintain efficiency, we apply\nLoRA[5] to the LLM decoder.\n\n3. Experiments\nTo validate the effectiveness of our Whisper-Aware LLM, we\nconducted a series of comprehensive experiments. We aimed to\nevaluate its performance on whispered speech recognition, its\ngeneral ASR capabilities, its robustness against hallucination,\nand the contribution of its core components.\n3.1. Experimental Setup\n3.1.1. Datasets and Metrics\nTraining Datasets. Our work leverages two strategically constructed datasets for different training phases to build a highly\nrobust model.\n• UPM Pre-training Corpus (for Stage 1): To ensure the\nUPM learns a comprehensive model of acoustic signals,\nwe constructed a large-scale, mixed-domain corpus. This\ncorpus includes General-Purpose Speech (a 3,000h subset\nof WenetSpeech[9], a 3,000h subset of GigaSpeech[10],\nAISHELL-1[11] and LibriSpeech[12]), Whispered Speech\n(wTIMIT[13], AISHELL6-Whisper[14]), and a 1,000h collection of pure noise.\n• Fine-tuning Set (for Stage 2 & 3): This set consists of\nthe training splits of AISHELL-1, LibriSpeech, wTIMIT,\nAISHELL6-Whisper, and a 200h subset of the pure noise\ndata to maintain robustness.\nEvaluation Datasets. We evaluate our model’s performance across three key dimensions:\n• Whispered Speech: The official test sets of AISHELL6Whisper [2] (Chinese) and wTIMIT [15] (English).\n• General-Purpose ASR: The test sets of AISHELL-1 and LibriSpeech to assess general competency.\n• Reliability and Hallucination: To specifically evaluate hallucination under adverse conditions, we created a Noise Hallucination Set containing 1000 audio clips. These clips consist\nof various non-speech sounds (e.g., machine hum, wind) and\nsegments with either no speech or very faint speech heavily\nmasked by noise, designed to be challenging for conventional\nVADs and prone to trigger false transcriptions.\nMetrics. The primary performance metrics are Character Error Rate (CER%) for Chinese and Word Error Rate\n(WER%)/CER% for English. On the Noise Hallucination Set,\nwe measure the Hallucination Rate (HR%), calculated as the\npercentage of audio files containing no transcribable speech that\nproduce a non-empty transcription.\n\n3.1.2. Implementation Details\n• Base Model: Our framework is built upon the Qwen2-Audio\nmodel [16]. It consists of an audio encoder, a lightweight\nprojector as the audio-LLM adapter, and a Qwen-7B LLM\n[17] backbone.\n• Uncertainty Perception Module (UPM): Our UPM is composed of a shared feature extractor (two 1D-CNN layers with\nGeLU and a single Transformer[18] encoder layer) followed\nby two task-specific heads for F0 prediction and masked\nspectrum reconstruction.\n• Training Details: Our three-stage strategy was implemented\nusing the AdamW[19] optimizer with a learning rate of\n1×10−5 . In Stage 3, the LLM was updated via LoRA\n(rank=64, alpha=32). The auxiliary loss weight was set to\nλaux = 0.1. Stage 1 ran for 100k steps, while Stages 2 and 3\neach ran for 20k steps.\n3.1.3. Compared Systems\nWe benchmark against internal baselines sharing the Qwen2Audio backbone and external state-of-the-art (SOTA) systems.\nThe systems S1-S7 are defined as: Whisper-v3 [20] (S1) and\nits fine-tuned version (S2); a fine-tuned Qwen2-Audio baseline\n(S3); and other recent SOTA ASR systems (S4-S7).\n3.2. Main Results\n3.2.1. Whispered Speech Accuracy\nWe first evaluate whispered speech accuracy on both Chinese\nand English benchmarks to demonstrate the cross-lingual effectiveness of our framework.\nAs detailed in Table 2, our model achieves a new state-ofthe-art on the challenging AISHELL6-Whisper test set. With a\nCER of only 1.31% on whispered speech, it significantly surpasses all compared systems, including the strong Seed-ASR\n(S7) which scored 1.58%. This represents a relative CER reduction of 17% over the previous best. Importantly, our model\nalso achieves the lowest error rate on normal speech (0.63%),\nindicating that its specialization for whispers does not compromise its general ASR capabilities.\nThe results on English benchmarks, presented in Table 1,\nfurther reinforce these findings. Our model consistently outperforms all baselines across both normal (N) and whispered\n(W) conditions for US and Singaporean (SG) accents. Notably,\nwhile other models show a dramatic increase in WER when\ntransitioning from normal to whispered speech, our model exhibits a much more graceful degradation. This demonstrates its\nenhanced robustness to the acoustic shift caused by whispering,\na direct result of its uncertainty-aware architecture.\n3.2.2. General ASR Performance\nTo explicitly verify that our model’s specialization for whispers does not impair its general capabilities, we summarize its\nperformance on standard, non-whispered benchmarks in Table\n3. Our model demonstrates highly competitive performance\nagainst state-of-the-art systems. On the AISHELL-1 test set,\nit achieves a strong CER of 1.34%, placing it on par with other\ntop-tier systems like Funasr (1.22%). On the LibriSpeech-clean\nset, our model obtains a robust WER of 1.91%, again showing performance in the same league as leading models. This\nconfirms that our uncertainty-aware framework successfully enhances robustness in challenging conditions without sacrificing\nits proficiency on high-quality, normal speech.\n\n\fTable 1: Detailed performance comparison on English benchmarks (WER%/CER%). Our model demonstrates superior performance\non both normal (N) and whispered (W) speech.\nWER (%)\n\nModel\n\nCER (%)\n\nNU S\n\nNSG\n\nWU S\n\nWSG\n\nNU S\n\nNSG\n\nWU S\n\nWSG\n\nS1: Whisper-v3\nS2: Whisper-v3 fine-tuned\nS3: Qwen2-Audio fine-tuned\nS4: Aishell6-whisper AVSR\nS5: Qwen3-ASR[6]\nS6: Funasr-ASR[7]\nS7: Seed-ASR[8]\n\n5.25\n5.58\n4.95\n4.50\n4.99\n4.65\n4.29\n\n8.67\n7.46\n6.85\n6.06\n7.26\n6.96\n6.51\n\n8.20\n7.95\n7.40\n6.35\n7.01\n8.48\n6.88\n\n19.03\n15.79\n13.50\n11.63\n16.46\n19.29\n12.95\n\n1.03\n0.93\n0.88\n0.73\n0.98\n0.91\n0.85\n\n2.50\n1.86\n1.65\n1.33\n1.85\n1.76\n1.62\n\n2.47\n2.37\n2.30\n1.95\n2.45\n2.88\n2.15\n\n8.85\n7.53\n6.20\n5.17\n7.11\n8.24\n6.03\n\nOurs\n\n4.15\n\n5.89\n\n5.92\n\n10.81\n\n0.70\n\n1.25\n\n1.85\n\n4.98\n\nTable 2: Performance on the AISHELL6-Whisper set (CER%).\nCER (%)\n\nModel\n\nNormal\n\nWhisper\n\nS1: Whisper-v3\nS2: Whisper-v3 fine-tuned\nS3: Qwen2-Audio fine-tuned\nS4: Aishell6-whisper AVSR\nS5: Qwen3-ASR\nS6: Funasr-ASR\nS7: Seed-ASR\n\n3.95\n1.62\n1.05\n1.11\n0.64\n0.72\n0.65\n\n18.93\n6.69\n3.98\n4.13\n3.79\n19.50\n1.58\n\nOurs\n\n0.63\n\n1.31\n\nTable 3: General ASR performance on standard benchmarks.\nModel\n\nAISHELL-1\n(CER%)\n\nLibriSpeech-clean\n(WER%)\n\nWhisper-v3\nSeed-ASR\nFunasr\n\n4.72\n1.63\n1.22\n\n1.86\n2.80\n1.51\n\nOurs\n\n1.34\n\n1.91\n\nTable 4: Reliability evaluation on the Noise Hallucination Set.\nModel\n\nHR(%)\n\nS3: Qwen2-Audio fine-tuned\nS5: Qwen3-ASR\nS6: Funasr-ASR\nS7: Seed-ASR\n\n35.5\n25.2\n29.3\n25.2\n\nOurs\n\n4.5\n\n3.3. Ablation of Model Components\nTo dissect the contribution of our proposed components, we\nconducted an ablation study on the AISHELL6-Whisper, with\nresults in Table 5. Starting from the S3 baseline (3.98%\nCER), using only the frame-wise Attention Modulation yields a\nclear improvement to 3.45%. The Global Instruction, however,\nproves to be the primary driver of performance, slashing the\nCER to 1.84% by providing high-level, strategic awareness of\nsignal quality. Finally, the Full Model combines both, achieving\nthe best performance at 1.31%.\nTable 5: Ablation of Confidence-Fused Decoding components\non AISHELL6-Whisper (Whisper subset).\nConfiguration\n\n3.2.3. Reliability and Hallucination\nA core contribution of our work is resolving the accuracyreliability trade-off. We test this on our curated Noise Hallucination Set, where an ideal system should produce no output.\nAs shown in Table 4, standard fine-tuned models exhibit a high\nHallucination Rate (HR). For instance, even strong baselines\nlike S7 and S5 incorrectly generate text for over 25% of the\nfiles, with the highest rate reaching 29.3% (S6). This highlights\ntheir tendency to “imagine” speech when faced with ambiguous\nsignals. In stark contrast, our Whisper-Aware LLM achieves\nan HR of only 4.5%, a substantial reduction. This remarkable\nimprovement demonstrates that by learning to perceive signal\nuncertainty, our model effectively learns when not to speak,\ndrastically mitigating the risk of generating spurious text from\nnon-speech audio. This reduction is primarily attributed to the\nGlobal Instruction mechanism, which guides the decoder, as\nquantified in our ablation study.\n\nBaseline (Vanilla Finetuning)\n+ Attention Modulation Only\n+ Global Instruction Only\n+ Full Model (Ours)\n\nCER(%)\n3.98\n3.45\n1.84\n1.31\n\n4. Conclusion\nIn this paper, we introduced the Whisper-Aware LLM, a framework designed to handle the uncertainty inherent in whispered\nspeech. Instead of just matching patterns, our approach teaches\nthe model to first perceive signal quality through targeted selfsupervised tasks. This learned awareness is then used by our\nConfidence-Fused Decoding mechanism to guide the LLM, allowing it to selectively trust the acoustic evidence it receives.\nOur experiments confirm the success of this strategy. The model\nachieves state-of-the-art results on whispered speech benchmarks and drastically reduces hallucinations, all while remaining a highly effective general-purpose ASR system.\n\n\f5. Generative AI Use Disclosure\nThe authors used generative AI tools solely for language editing\nand grammar polishing. All technical content, methodology,\nexperimental design, and analysis were conducted entirely by\nthe authors.\n\n6. References\n[1] Z. Lin, T. Patel, and O. Scharenborg, “Improving whispered\nspeech recognition performance using pseudo-whispered based\ndata augmentation,” in 2023 IEEE Workshop on Automatic Speech\nRecognition and Understanding (ASRU), 2023, pp. 1–8.\n[2] C. Li, F. Su, J. Liu, H. Bu, Y. Wan, H. Suo, and M. Li,\n“Aishell6-whisper: A chinese mandarin audio-visual whisper\nspeech dataset with speech recognition baselines,” arXiv preprint\narXiv:2403.19833, 2024.\n[3] P. R. Gudepu, G. P. Vadisetti, A. Niranjan, K. Saranu, R. Sarma,\nM. A. B. Shaik, and P. Paramasivam, “Whisper augmented endto-end/hybrid speech recognition system—cyclegan approac,” in\nProc. Interspeech 2020, 2020, pp. 2302–2306.\n[4] D. E. Rumelhart, G. E. Hinton, and R. J. Williams, “Learning\nrepresentations by back-propagating errors,” nature, vol. 323, no.\n6088, pp. 533–536, 1986.\n[5] E. J. Hu, Y. Shen, P. Wallis, Z. Allen-Zhu, Y. Li, S. Wang,\nL. Wang, W. Chen et al., “Lora: Low-rank adaptation of large\nlanguage models.” Iclr, vol. 1, no. 2, p. 3, 2022.\n[6] X. Shi, X. Wang, Z. Guo, Y. Wang, P. Zhang, X. Zhang, Z. Guo,\nH. Hao, Y. Xi, B. Yang et al., “Qwen3-asr technical report,” arXiv\npreprint arXiv:2601.21337, 2025.\n[7] K. An, Y. Chen, Z. Chen, C. Deng, Z. Du, C. Gao, Z. Gao,\nB. Gong, X. Li, Y. Li et al., “Fun-asr technical report,” arXiv\npreprint arXiv:2509.12508, 2025.\n[8] Y. Bai, J. Chen, J. Chen, W. Chen, Z. Chen, C. Ding, L. Dong,\nQ. Dong, Y. Du, K. Gao et al., “Seed-asr: Understanding diverse\nspeech and contexts with llm-based speech recognition,” arXiv\npreprint arXiv:2407.04675, 2024.\n[9] B. Zhang, H. Lv, P. Guo, Q. Shao, C. Yang, L. Xie, X. Xu, H. Bu,\nX. Chen, C. Zeng et al., “Wenetspeech: A 10000+ hours multidomain mandarin corpus for speech recognition,” in ICASSP\n2022-2022 IEEE International Conference on Acoustics, Speech\nand Signal Processing (ICASSP). IEEE, 2022, pp. 6182–6186.\n[10] G. Chen, S. Chai, G. Wang, J. Du, W.-Q. Zhang, C. Weng, D. Su,\nD. Povey, J. Trmal, J. Zhang et al., “Gigaspeech: An evolving,\nmulti-domain asr corpus with 10,000 hours of transcribed audio,”\narXiv preprint arXiv:2106.06909, 2021.\n[11] H. Bu, J. Du, X. Na, B. Wu, and H. Zheng, “Aishell-1: An opensource mandarin speech corpus and a speech recognition baseline,” in 2017 20th conference of the oriental chapter of the international coordinating committee on speech databases and speech\nI/O systems and assessment (O-COCOSDA). IEEE, 2017, pp.\n1–5.\n[12] V. Panayotov, G. Chen, D. Povey, and S. Khudanpur, “Librispeech: an asr corpus based on public domain audio books,”\nin 2015 IEEE international conference on acoustics, speech and\nsignal processing (ICASSP). IEEE, 2015, pp. 5206–5210.\n[13] B. P. Lim, Computational differences between whispered and nonwhispered speech. University of Illinois at Urbana-Champaign,\n2011.\n[14] C. Li, F. Su, J. Liu, H. Bu, Y. Wan, H. Suo, and M. Li,\n“Aishell6-whisper: A chinese mandarin audio-visual whisper\nspeech dataset with speech recognition baselines,” arXiv preprint\narXiv:2509.23833, 2025.\n[15] B. P. Lim, “Computational differences between whispered and\nnon-whispered speech,” Ph.D. dissertation, University of Illinois\nat Urbana-Champaign, 2011.\n\n[16] Y. Chu, J. Xu, Q. Yang, H. Wei, X. Wei, Z. Guo, Y. Leng, Y. Lv,\nJ. He, J. Lin et al., “Qwen2-audio technical report,” arXiv preprint\narXiv:2407.10759, 2024.\n[17] J. Bai, S. Bai, Y. Chu, Z. Cui, K. Dang, X. Deng, Y. Fan, W. Ge,\nY. Han, F. Huang et al., “Qwen technical report,” arXiv preprint\narXiv:2309.16609, 2023.\n[18] A. Vaswani, N. Shazeer, N. Parmar, J. Uszkoreit, L. Jones, A. N.\nGomez, Ł. Kaiser, and I. Polosukhin, “Attention is all you need,”\nAdvances in neural information processing systems, vol. 30, 2017.\n[19] I. Loshchilov and F. Hutter, “Decoupled weight decay regularization,” arXiv preprint arXiv:1711.05101, 2017.\n[20] A. Radford, J. W. Kim, T. Xu, G. Brockman, C. McLeavey, and\nI. Sutskever, “Robust speech recognition via large-scale weak\nsupervision,” in International conference on machine learning.\nPMLR, 2023, pp. 28 492–28 518.\n\n\f", "numLines": 653, "startLine": 1, "totalLines": 653}}}
